[repulsive.end]
#endif   :#ifdef HYDRONIUM

[segment.end]
--------------------------------------------------------------------
\end{verbatim}
The functional forms for the repulsive interactions are defined in the papers discussing the MS-EVB3 model.\cite{WuChenWangPaesaniVoth2008}
The repulsive interactions between oxygen atoms of hydronium and water is defined to be
\begin{equation}
V^{rep}_{OO_k} = B e^{-b(R_{OO_k} - d^0_{OO})} \sum_{j=1}^3 e^{-b' {\bf q}^2_{H_jO_k}},
\end{equation}
where the proton transfer reaction coordinate is defined to be
\begin{equation}
{\bf q}_{H_jO_k} = \frac{1}{2}({\bf r}_O + {\bf r}_{O_k}) - {\bf r}_{H_j}
\end{equation}
and the sum over $j$ is over the three hydronium hydrogens. A similar interaction is defined for the repulsion between the hydronium hydrogens and water oxygens.
\begin{equation}
V^{rep}_{HO_k} = C e^{-c(R_{HO_k} - d^0_{OH})}
\end{equation}
Both of these repulsive interactions are scaled using a switching function, so that they are zero beyond a specified distance.  The piecewise continuous switching function is defined as follows.
\begin{displaymath}
  S(r)=  \left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
      1 & r < r_s \\ 
      1- \frac{(r - r_s)^2(3r_c - r_s - 2r)}{(r_c - r_s)^3} & r_s < r < r_c \\
      0 & r_c < r
    \end{array}\right.
\end{displaymath}
This switching function is only piecewise continuous and does not have continuous first derivatives at the boundaries.


