\section{FitEVB}
\label{sec:Fitevb}

There is also a 'FitEVB.docx' file that has been prepared with current information that may not be contained here. It is recommended that the latest version of FitEVB be downloaded from svn repository.
\begin{verbatim}
svn co https://w3.rcc.uchicago.edu/svn/fitevb/trunk FitEVB
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Input Files}
\label{sec:Fitevb_input}
The main directory that the {\bf FitEVB} will be run should contain
several input files to control the running of the code and a {\bf
  DATA} directory that contains all reference and checkpoint
data.

\subsubsection{ga.in}
When using the genetic algorithm for optimizing model parameters, this
input file is used to initialize several control variables. This input
file contains the control statements and identifies which parameters
will be optimized.
\begin{verbatim}
NGENOME    160000
NSELECT        50
RMUTATE        10
OUTPUT         10
TOTAL           0
\end{verbatim}
The following is a brief explanation for all acceptable keywords.
\begin{enumerate}
\item{{\bf NGENOME}: The total number of parameter vectors that will
    be maintained. This is the sum of the number of children and
    parents. The default value is zero.}
\item{{\bf NSELECT}: The number of parameter vectors (parents) used to
    generate new parameter vectors (children). The total number of
    children is NGENOME minus NSELECT. The default value is zero.}
\item{{\bf RMUTATE}: The total number of mutatations made at each
    step.  For each mutation, an element (gene) of a parameter vector
    (child) is selected at random and replaced with a random value
    within the specified range. The default value is zero.}
\item{{\bf OUTPUT}: This is the frequency with which a restart file is
    written to ``fit.restart''. The default value is 60.}
\item{{\bf TOTAL}: This is the frequency with which an output file is
    written to ``ga.total'' with some general information on how the
    optimization is proceeding. The default value is 10.}
\item{{\bf SCREEN}: When given the ``OFF'' option, this command turns
    of the writing of output to standard output. The default value is
    ``YES''.}
\end{enumerate}
The defaults for most parameters are set to zero, so it is advisable
to set all of the keywords listed in the example above.

\subsubsection{fit.inp}
This file contains a list of each model parameter that is to be
optimized in the calculation. This file is divided into sections for
each contribution to the energies and forces that model paramters will
be optimized.  Currently, this would correspond to different sections
for fitting model parameters of the off-diagonal couplings and
short-range repulsions in an MS-EVB3 model. The example below is used
to fit the off-diagonal coupling using the functional form of the
MS-EVB3 model.\cite{WuChenWangPaesaniVoth2008}
\begin{verbatim}
FIT  ARQ2  H3O_H2O
     -2.0     3.0    : g
     -0.5     2.5    : P
    -10.0    10.0    : k
     -1.0     5.0    : D    
    -10.0    10.0    : beta
     -2.0     3.5    : R00
      5.0    15.0    : P1
     -5.0    15.0    : alpha   
     -3.0     3.0    : r00
    -65.0   -25.0    : Vij_const
\end{verbatim}
The first line of each section contains the 'FIT' command followed by
the functional form to be fit and the name of the interaction given in
the checkpoint files generated by the Raptor code.  Each line
afterwards contains the lower and upper limits to be sampled by the
optimization algorithm.  The following is a list of the currently
implemented functional forms.  The list of parameters for each
function can be found at the top of the respective compute() function
in the {\bf FitEVB} source code.
\\ \\{\bf Off-diagonals}
\begin{enumerate}
\item{{\bf ARQ}: Fit a general non-symmetric off-diagonal coupling using the
    MS-EVB3 functional form without exchange charges. This is
    used when fitting amino acid models.}
\item{{\bf ARQ2}: Fit a general symmetric off-diagonal coupling for the
    MS-EVB3 functional form without exchange charges. This is
    used when fitting models for the excess proton.}
\item{{\bf ARQ3}: Fit a general symmetric off-diagonal coupling for the
    MS-EVB3 functional form with nonzero exchange charges. This is
    used when fitting models for the excess proton.}
\item{{\bf ARQ4}: Fit a general symmetric off-diagonal coupling for the
    MS-EVB3 functional form with nonzero exchange charges. This is
    used when fitting models for the hydroxide ion.}
\item{{\bf DAH}: }
\item{{\bf DA\_Gaussian}: Fit a simple Gaussian off-diagonal as function of separation of two atoms without exchange charges.}
\item{{\bf Table}: }
\end{enumerate}

{\bf Diabatic Shifts}
\begin{enumerate}
\item{{\bf REP1}: Fit in form of short-ranged repulsions using the MS-EVB3
    functional form when fitting models for the excess proton.}
\item{{\bf REP2}: Fit in form of short-ranged repulsions for the MS-EVB3 functional
    form when fitting models for the hydroxide ion.}
\item{{\bf VII}: Fit as a constant diabiatic shift. This is typically used
    for assymmetric reactions, such as those involving amino acids.}
\item{{\bf Morse}: Fit a diabatic correction with a Morse potential (specialized).}
\item{{\bf Pair}: Fit a diabatic correction with a pairwise LJ potential (specialized).}
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{INDEX}
This file contains a list of file names that the {\bf FitEVB} code
will use to search for input for each configuration in the training
set. The first line contains the number of configurations that will be
included.  This is followed by a list of filenames with one per
line. Below is a simple example.
\begin{verbatim}
4
1
TWO
333
FouR
\end{verbatim}
With this list of filenames, the {\bf FitEVB} code will search for the
following files in the {\bf /DATA} directory.
\begin{verbatim}
CHK_1       REF_1
CHK_TWO     REF_TWO
CHK_333     REF_333
CHK_FouR    REF_FouR
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{ENERGY}
This file contains a list of potential energies for each of the
configuration names given in the INDEX file. The first line indicates
which configuration is to be used as the zero of energy. In the
example below, this would be the second configuration. 
\begin{verbatim}
ZERO   TWO
 1    -1904.7238 
 TWO  -1905.3051
 333  -1903.3633
 FouR -1904.8856
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{WEIGHT}
This file controls the different weighting options for the evaluation
of the residual used in the minimization procedure. Different weights
can be given for energies/forces, configurations, and even individual
atom indices. An example of this file is given below.
\begin{verbatim}
energy 0.5
point * 1.47736141596464
atom  * 1 0.708813792540141
atom  * 2 2.04051115090347
atom  * 3 2.42977708218165
\end{verbatim}
The first line indicates the relative weight used for energies and
forces. In the example below, the energies are included with a 50\%
weight.  The statement 'energy 0.0' would indicate that only the
forces are used in the contruction of the residual. To turn off any
special weighting for the configurations and atoms, the following
could be used for the {\bf WEIGHT} file.
\begin{verbatim}
energy 0.0
point *   1.0
atom  * * 1.0
\end{verbatim}

\subsubsection{/opt}
The {\bf opt} directory contains the coordinates for the atoms in all configurations. The names of the files to be read are controlled by the names given in the first column of the {\bf metadata} file. In this example, the code will expect to find the files {\bf 1.xyz}, {\bf 2.xyz}, $\ldots$, {\bf 11.xyz}. Each file contains the coordinates of all atoms in the order specified in the {\bf index} file and is formatted as in the following example.
\begin{verbatim}
 C                 -0.00120000    0.00000000    0.00060000
 H                 -0.00240000   -0.00240000    1.08970000
 C                  1.45030000    0.00510000   -0.53940000
 N                 -0.65620000    1.25430000   -0.40670000
 H                 -0.41530000    2.05310000    0.15940000
 N                  2.46640000   -0.00260000    0.35170000
 H                  3.37490000   -0.00260000   -0.09170000
 C                  2.41710000   -0.12390000    1.80950000
\end{verbatim}
The first character on each line is read by the code, but is not used for anything. The coordinates have units corresponding to those defined in the Lammps input script (\AA\ if "real" units are used).

\subsubsection{/force}
The {\bf force} directory contains the reference forces for all atoms in all configurations.  The names of the files to be read are controlled by the names given in the first column of the {\bf metafile} file. In this example, the code will expected to find the files {\bf 1.force}, {\bf 2.force}, $\ldots$, {\bf 11.force}. Each file contains the forces of all atoms in the order specified in the {\bf index} file and is formatted just like the files containing the coordinates. Again, the first character on each line is ignored by the code and the forces have units corresponding to those defined in the Lammps input script (kcal/mol per \AA\ if "real" units are used).

\subsubsection{ga.chk}
This file contains the initial genomes use to start the optimization.

