\subsection{Biasing Potentials Applied to CEC}
\label{sec:Algorithm_Umb}
This section will discuss the implementation of biasing potentials
applied to the center of excess charge (CEC) in MS-EVB simulations.
The forces due to the biasing potential will involve derivatives of
the ground state MS-EVB eigenvector with respect the particle
coordinates.\cite{TepperVoth2005,KofingerDellago2008}

The position of the CEC is defined as follows
\begin{equation}
{\bf r}_{CEC} = \sum_{j=1}^{N_{states}} c_{0i}^2\ {\bf r}_{COC,j},
\end{equation}
where $c_{i0}$ are the coefficients of the ground state eigenvector
and ${\bf r}_{COC,j}$ is the position of the center of charge (COC)
for the $j$th MS-EVB state.  The forces due to the biasing potential,
$V({\bf r}_{CEC})$ can be calculated using the chain rule.
\begin{equation} {\bf F}_j = -\frac{\partial V({\bf
      r}_{CEC})}{\partial {\bf r}_j} = -\frac{\partial V({\bf
      r}_{CEC})}{\partial {\bf r}_{CEC}} \frac{\partial {\bf
      r}_{CEC}}{\partial {\bf r}_j}
\end{equation}
The first derivative can be evaluated straightforwardly when the form
of the biasing potential has been defined. Using the definition for
the CEC coordinate, the second derivative can be written.
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial {\bf r}_{CEC}}{\partial {\bf r}_l} = \frac{\partial
}{\partial {\bf r}_l}  \sum_{j=1}^{N_{states}} c_{0i}^2\ {\bf
  r}_{COC,j} = \sum_{j=1}^{N_{states}} \bigg [ 2 \frac{\partial
  c_{0i}}{\partial {\bf r}_l}\ {\bf r}_{COC,j} + c_{0i}^2
\frac{\partial {\bf r}_{COC,j}}{\partial {\bf r}_l} \bigg ]
\end{equation}
The derivatives involving the COC coordinate are straightforwardly
evaluated using the definition for the COC coodinate.
\begin{equation} {\bf r}_{COC,j} = \frac{1}{\sum_{k=1}^{N_{A,COC}}
    |q_{k,j}| } \sum_{k=1}^{N_{A,COC}} |q_{k,j}| {\bf r}_k,
\end{equation}
where the sum is over all atoms in the reactive molecule.  The derivative involving the ground state eigenvector coefficients can be derived using first order perturbation theory.\cite{TepperVoth2005,KofingerDellago2008} 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\partial c_{0i}}{\partial {\bf x}_k} = -\sum_{j \ne 0}^{N_S} \sum_{lm}^{N_S} \frac{ {\bf F}_k^{lm} c_{jl}c_{0m} }{E_0 - E_j} c_{ji}
\end{equation}
In Raptor, the derivatives of the COC coordinates are evaluated in the {\bf EVB\_CEC::decompose\_force()} function.  The derivatives of the eigenvector coefficients are calculated in the {\bf   EVB\_CEC::partial\_C\_N3()} function. In this function, the dummy arrays are defined in the following sequence.
\begin{eqnarray}
{\bf array1}[l] &=& -\sum_{m=0}^{N_S} {\bf F}_k^{lm} c_{0m} \\
{\bf array2}[j] &=& \sum_{l=0}^{N_S} {\bf array1}[l] c_{jl} = -\sum_{lm}^{N_S} {\bf F}_k^{lm} c_{jl} c_{0m} \\
{\bf array1}[j] &=& \frac{ {\bf array2}[j] }{E_j - E_0} = -\sum_{lm}^{N_S} \frac{{\bf F}_k^{lm} c_{jl} c_{0m}}{E_j - E_0}  \\
\frac{\partial c_{0i}}{{\partial \bf r}_k} = {\bf dcdx}[i] &=& \sum_{j\ne 0}^{N_S} {\bf array1}[j] c_{jl} = -\sum_{j\ne 0}^{N_S}\sum_{lm}^{N_S} \frac{{\bf F}_k^{lm} c_{jl} c_{0m}}{E_j - E_0} c_{ji}
\end{eqnarray}

