\subsection{Ewald}
\label{sec:Algorithm_Ewald}
The derivation of the necessary expressions can be found in any standard solid state physics text. The expression for the potential energy is 
\begin{eqnarray}
V &=& \frac{2\pi}{V} \sum_{k\ne 0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{k^2} e^{-k^2/4\alpha^2} \bigg | \sum_{j=1}^N q_j e^{-i {\bf k}.{\bf r}_j}\bigg |^2 + \sum_{i<j} \frac{q_i q_j}{r_{ij}} {\rm erfc}(\alpha r_{ij}) - \frac{\alpha}{\sqrt{\pi}} \sum_{j=1}^N q_j^2 -\\
	& & \sum_{\begin{subarray}{c}i<j\\ij\ \in\ molecule\end{subarray}} \frac{q_i q_j}{r_{ij}} {\rm erf}(\alpha r_{ij}) + \frac{2\pi}{(2\epsilon_S + 1)V} \bigg | \sum_{j=1}^N q_j {\bf r}_j \bigg |^2 - \frac{\pi}{V2\alpha^2}Q_{TOT}^2
\end{eqnarray}
A factor of $1/4\pi\epsilon_0$ has been factored out of the expression and multiplication by the {\bf qqrd2e} variable in Lammps accounts for this. The first three terms correspond to the k-space, real space, and the self-interaction correction to the k-space term.  The fourth term corrects for the intramolecular contributions that were included in the k-space calculation which are normally omitted. The second from last term accounts for dipolar interactions with the surrounding environment.  Typically, conducting (or "tin-foil") boundary conditions are assumed, $\epsilon_S = \infty$. If vacuum, $\epsilon_S = 0$ boundary conditions are used, then one must be careful to remove discontinous jumps in energy as particles move across the periodic boundaries. The last term is a correction for non-neutral simulation cells.

Some useful expressions for the calculation of the forces follow.
\begin{equation}
\bigg | \sum_{j=1}^N q_j e^{-i {\bf k}.{\bf r}_j}\bigg |^2 = \sum_{i,j}^N q_i q_j e^{-i {\bf k}.{\bf r}_{ij}}
\end{equation}

\begin{eqnarray}
{\rm erf}(x) &=& \frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}} \int_0^x dt e^{-t^2} \\
{\rm erfc}(x) &=& 1 - {\rm erf}(x)
\end{eqnarray}

\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{d}{dx} {\rm erf}(x) &=& \frac{d}{dx} \bigg \{ \frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}} \int_0^x dt e^{-t^2}\bigg \} = \frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}} e^{-x^2} \\
\frac{d}{dx} {\rm erfc}(x) &=& -\frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}} e^{-x^2}
\end{eqnarray}

The particle-particle contribution to the force on the {\em m}th atom due to the k-space interactions is
\begin{equation}
{\bf F}_m = 2 q_m \frac{2\pi}{V} \sum_{j=1}^N q_j \sum_{k\ne 0}^\infty \frac{1}{k^2} e^{-k^2/4\alpha^2} {\rm sin}[{\bf k}.({\bf r}_j-{\bf r}_m)] {\bf k}
\end{equation}

The particle-particle contribution to the force on the {\em j}th atom due to the real space interactions is
\begin{equation}
{\bf F}_j = q_j \sum_{i\ne j}^N \frac{q_i}{r_{ij}^3} \bigg \{ {\rm erfc}(\alpha r_{ij}) + \frac{2\alpha r_{ij}}{\sqrt{\pi}}e^{-\alpha^2 r_{ij}^2} \bigg \} {\bf r}_{ij}
\end{equation}

The particle-particle contribution to the force on the {\em j}th atom due to the intramolecular corrections is
\begin{equation}
{\bf F}_j = q_j \sum_{i\ne j}^N \frac{q_i}{r_{ij}^3} \bigg \{ -{\rm erf}(\alpha r_{ij}) + \frac{2\alpha r_{ij}}{\sqrt{\pi}}e^{-\alpha^2 r_{ij}^2} \bigg \} {\bf r}_{ij}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{LAMMPS}
The variable ``qqr2de = qqrd2e / dielectric'' is the Coulomb constant used for calculating electrostatic energies and forces.  The relative dielectric constant is defined to be one unless specified otherwise in the Lammps input script. 
\begin{equation}
qqr2e = \frac{1}{4\pi \epsilon_0} = 8.9876\ {\rm x}\ 10^9 J\ m\ C^{-2},
\end{equation}
where the vacuum permittivity, $\epsilon_0$, is $8.8542\ {\rm x}\
10^{-12} F\ m$.  The constant ``qqr2de'' is then defined to be the
electrostatic energy between two point charges separated by one
Angstrom.
\begin{eqnarray}
  qqr2de &=& \frac{1}{4\pi \epsilon_0} \frac{q_1 q_2}{r_{12}} =
  \frac{1}{4\pi \epsilon_0} \frac{(1.0e)(1.0e)}{1 \AA} \\
  &=& 8.9876\ {\rm x}\ 10^9 J\ m\ C^{-2} \frac{(1.6022\ {\rm x}\ 10^{-19}\
    C)^2}{10^{-10}\ m} \\
  &=& 2.3072\ {\rm x}\ 10^{-18} J
\end{eqnarray}
This value is then divided by Avogadro's number and converted to
kilocalories.
\begin{equation}
  qqr2de = \frac{(2.3072\ {\rm x}\ 10^{-18} J)\ (6.022\ {\rm x}\
    10^{23} mol^{-1})}{4184\ J/kcal} = 332.064\ kcal/mol
\end{equation}
This is the value defined in the {\bf Update::set\_units()} function in Lammps when using ``real'' units.

The definitions for some other variables and arrays are defined below.
\begin{itemize}
\item{g\_ewald: Screening parameter $\alpha$ used in the equations for the Ewald energies and forces.}
\item{kcount: Total number of wavevectors included in calculation.}
\item{kmax: Largest integer component of wavevector included in calculation.}
\item{kmax3d: Number of  wavevectors in sphere with radius defined by kmax.}
\item{kxvecs[k]: x-component of $k$th wavevector stored as integer.}
\item{kyvecs[k]: y-component of $k$th wavevector stored as integer.}
\item{kzvecs[k]: z-component of $k$th wavevector stored as integer.}
\item{ug[k]: Wavevector-dependent prefactor calculated in the Ewald::coeffs() function.
\begin{equation}
ug[k] = \frac{4\pi}{V{\bf k}^2} e^{-{\bf k}^2/4\alpha^2}
\end{equation}
}
\item{eg[k]: 3d force contribution from $k$th wavevector calculated in the Ewald::coeffs() function.
\begin{equation}
eg[k] = 2 {\bf k} ug[k]
\end{equation}
}
\end{itemize}

The Ewald::eik\_dot\_r() function calculates the partial structure factor from particles local to a processor. The total structure factor is accumulated on all processors using MPI\_Allreduce() calls in the Ewald::compute() function.
\begin{itemize}
\item{sfacrl[n]: The real contribution to the structure factor for the $n$th wavevector summed over all particles local to the processor.
\begin{equation}
sfacrl[n] = \sum_{i=1}^{N_{local}} q_i \cos({\bf k}_n.{\bf r}_i)
\end{equation}
}
\item{sfacim[n]: The imaginary contribution to the structure factor for the $n$th wavevector summover over all particles local to the processor.
\begin{equation}
sfacim[n] = \sum_{i=1}^{N_{local}} q_i \sin({\bf k}_n.{\bf r}_i)
\end{equation}
}
\item{sfacrl\_all[k]: The real contribution to the structure factor summed over all particles for the $k$th wavevector.}
\item{sfacim\_all[k]: The imaginary contribution to the structure factor summec over all particles for the $k$th wavevector.}
\item{cs[{\bf k},i]: The term $\cos({\bf k}.{\bf r}_i)$ for each particle stored as an array for each of the xyz components.}
\item{sn[{\bf k},i]: The term $\sin({\bf k}.{\bf r}_i)$ for each particle stored as an array for each of the xyz components.}
\end{itemize}
The contributions from each wavevector are accumulated \red{(?)} using standard trigonometric identities in the eik\_dot\_r() function.
\begin{eqnarray}
\sin(x\pm y) &=& \sin(x)\cos(y)\pm \cos(x)\sin(y) \\
\cos(x\pm y) &=& \cos(x)\cos(y)\mp \sin(x)\sin(y)
\end{eqnarray}
The Ewald::compute() function calculates the electrostatic energies and forces. The self-interaction and non-neutral corrections are added to energy in the compute() function before the result is multiplied by the variable {\bf qqrd2e}.

\subsubsection{Raptor}
\label{sec:Algorithm_Ewald_Raptor}

For MS-EVB simulations, the partial structure factor for the environment atoms is calculated once and stored at the beginning of an MD step.  The electrostatic energies for each of the diagonal elements are then calculated by calculating the partial structure factor for the reactive complex atoms and adding this to the environment contribution.  The same is done for the off-diagonal elements and this leads to significant savings as opposed to recalculaing the entire lattice sum for each nonzero element of the MS-EVB Hamiltonian.  After the MS-EVB Hamiltonian has been diagonalized, the electrostatic forces due to the reactive complex atoms are then calculating using an effective set of charges.  With this algorithm, the electrostatic forces between complex and environment atoms only need to be calculated once.\cite{SmondyrevVoth2002}


